## Method and reasoning
Sort edges by weight and accept an edge exactly when it joins two different current components. Such an edge has minimum weight across the cut defined by one of those components, so some optimal forest contains the accepted choices. A self-loop never crosses a cut. Accumulate in 64 bits.

If an optimal forest omits the chosen edge, adding it creates a cycle in its original connected component. The path on that cycle crosses the same cut via another edge of at least the chosen weight. Exchanging those edges preserves optimality and all earlier choices.

## Walk through the sample
The loop of weight −5 connects no components. The forest accepts edges of weights −2 and 1 on vertices 1–3, plus 3 on vertices 4–5. The total is 2 across two components.

## Complexity
Time O(m log(m+1)+n), space O(n+m).

## Reference implementation
~~~
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

class DSU {
    vector<int> parent, size;

  public:
    explicit DSU(int n) : parent(n + 1), size(n + 1, 1) {
        iota(parent.begin(), parent.end(), 0);
    }
    int find(int v) {
        while (parent[v] != v) {
            parent[v] = parent[parent[v]];
            v = parent[v];
        }
        return v;
    }
    int count(int v) { return size[find(v)]; }
    bool unite(int u, int v) {
        u = find(u);
        v = find(v);
        if (u == v)
            return false;
        if (size[u] < size[v])
            swap(u, v);
        size[u] += size[v];
        parent[v] = u;
        return true;
    }
};

struct Edge {
    int u, v, w;
};
int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);
    int n, m;
    cin >> n >> m;
    vector<Edge> edges(m);
    for (auto &e : edges)
        cin >> e.u >> e.v >> e.w;
    sort(edges.begin(), edges.end(), [](Edge a, Edge b) { return a.w < b.w; });
    DSU d(n);
    long long total = 0;
    for (auto e : edges)
        if (d.unite(e.u, e.v)) {
            --n;
            total += e.w;
        }
    cout << total << ' ' << n << '\n';
}
